{
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "To distinguish small details, the angle between the directions from the eye to their edges, called the visual angle, needs to be large enough.\nIf the eye could adjust its focus without limit, bringing the same object closer would keep increasing this angle.\nThe eye's focusing adjustment has a limit, however, so an object brought too close may look blurred even though its visual angle is larger."
    },
    {
      "index": 1,
      "blockIndex": 1,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Optical instruments such as magnifying glasses can increase the visual angle and allow relaxed focusing when the ray arrangement and the eye's focusing state are suitable.\nThe relationship in Figure 185 shows how a larger visual angle corresponds to a larger retinal image in the same eye."
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "blockIndex": 1,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "For small angles, the ratio of retinal image sizes is approximately the ratio of visual angles.\nAn instrument can therefore spread details farther apart on the retina without making the object itself larger."
    },
    {
      "index": 3,
      "blockIndex": 2,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Angular magnification is the visual angle seen through an instrument divided by the angle seen when viewing the same object without it.\nIt compares the angle between the directions from the eye to the object's edges with and without the instrument, so the unaided viewing distance must also be specified."
    },
    {
      "index": 4,
      "blockIndex": 3,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "For a small angle, the unaided visual angle (Figure 188) can be approximated by object height divided by viewing distance, written in the source as $\\varphi=\\frac{h}{d_0}$ (6.20).\nHere $d_0=25$ cm is the reference distance of distinct vision, and $h$ is the object's height or the linear size being compared."
    },
    {
      "index": 5,
      "blockIndex": 3,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "This distance is a conventional reference in elementary optics, not the same nearest clear viewing distance for everyone.\nThe angle must be expressed in radians, an angle unit, rather than inserting a value in degrees directly, and the lengths must use matching units."
    },
    {
      "index": 6,
      "blockIndex": 8,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "A magnifying glass is a simple way to increase the visual angle when examining a small object.\nIt may be a converging lens or a lens group with a short focal length $F$, commonly no more than $10$ cm in the source's description, rather than a strict limit for every magnifying glass."
    },
    {
      "index": 7,
      "blockIndex": 8,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "We first use an ideal thin-lens model, placing the magnifying glass near the eye and the object in its focal plane.\nThe focal plane passes through the focus perpendicular to the optical axis, and an object there sends light from each point through the lens as an approximately parallel bundle (Figure 189)."
    },
    {
      "index": 8,
      "blockIndex": 8,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "Bundles from different object points can travel in different directions, so the details still enter the eye from different directions.\nAn eye that can already focus distant objects with its focusing adjustment relaxed can bring these approximately parallel bundles to focus on the retina without an extra adjustment."
    },
    {
      "index": 9,
      "blockIndex": 8,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "For this arrangement and the small-angle approximation, the angle seen through the magnifying glass is written as $\\varphi_1=\\frac{h}{F}$.\nThis is an approximate formula for the angle at the eye and does not describe every real lens exactly."
    },
    {
      "index": 10,
      "blockIndex": 9,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "By the definition of angular magnification, divide the magnifier's visual angle by the unaided visual angle to obtain $\\Gamma=\\frac{\\varphi_1}{\\varphi}$ (6.21).\nThe comparison still uses the same object and the previously specified unaided viewing distance."
    },
    {
      "index": 11,
      "blockIndex": 10,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "The source's substitution instruction names $\\varphi_1$ and $\\varphi_2$, but the latter symbol is not clearly defined here.\nWe retain the source's calculation as $\\Gamma=\\frac{h/F}{h/d_0}=\\frac{d_0}{F}$ (6.22)."
    },
    {
      "index": 12,
      "blockIndex": 10,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "In this written calculation, the object height cancels between numerator and denominator, leaving the comparison distance divided by the focal length.\nBecause the second symbol is not defined here, we cannot yet tell which angle it means, but we can follow the calculation above without treating it as another already explained angle."
    },
    {
      "index": 13,
      "blockIndex": 11,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Moving the object slightly inward from the focal plane toward the lens can give a somewhat greater magnification than the original arrangement.\nThe light entering the eye is no longer the same parallel-bundle arrangement, so the eye needs to adjust its focus to see clearly.\nThis compares magnification with focusing demand, rather than applying the relaxed-viewing formula to every object position."
    },
    {
      "index": 14,
      "blockIndex": 12,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "The source notes that a very short-focal-length single magnifier is often small and awkward to view through, placing practical limits on its usefulness.\nIts claim that only a small-diameter lens can have a short focal length is not a universal rule, because how strongly its surfaces curve and the lens material also affect focal length."
    },
    {
      "index": 15,
      "blockIndex": 12,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "A microscope works in two stages, first using the objective, near the object, to form an enlarged real image.\nLight actually meets at a real image, so this intermediate image can become the object for the next viewing stage."
    },
    {
      "index": 16,
      "blockIndex": 12,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "The eyepiece, near the eye, then acts like a magnifying glass for that intermediate image and further increases the angle through which its details are seen.\nAn instrument combining an objective and eyepiece this way is called a microscope."
    },
    {
      "index": 17,
      "blockIndex": 12,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "This lesson represents these parts with simple lenses, while actual objectives and eyepieces may contain several lenses and use other optical arrangements.\nThe important sequence here is an intermediate real image followed by eyepiece viewing, not a claim that the simplified structure describes every microscope completely."
    },
    {
      "index": 18,
      "blockIndex": 13,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "In this converging thin-lens model, an object between the objective's focus and the position at twice its focal length forms an enlarged real image on the other side.\nPlace the eyepiece so that this intermediate image lies in the eyepiece's focal plane."
    },
    {
      "index": 19,
      "blockIndex": 13,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Light from each intermediate image point then leaves the eyepiece as an approximately parallel bundle, so an eye able to see distant objects with relaxed focusing needs no extra adjustment.\nFigure 190 represents the light from object point $A$ with two rays meeting at intermediate real-image point $A_1$."
    },
    {
      "index": 20,
      "blockIndex": 13,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "The light is then refracted by the eyepiece into the eye, whose own optical system forms the retinal image at $A_2$.\nThe object, intermediate real image and retinal image must therefore be distinguished as different positions along the light path."
    },
    {
      "index": 21,
      "blockIndex": 13,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "The microscope increases the visual angle of details, allowing small parts that were difficult to distinguish without it to be separated.\nThe source calls those details overlapping during unaided viewing, meaning that their differences cannot be resolved, not that every detail's image is literally at the same point."
    }
  ],
